  "Question": "How many pairs of positive integers \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le100\\) satisfy the equation \\[ \\frac{a+b^{-1}}{a^{-1}+b}=13? \\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(5\\)",
    "C": "\\(7\\)",
    "D": "\\(9\\)",
    "E": "\\(13\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Combining the reciprocals reveals the same expression in the numerator and denominator. Since \\(a,b>0\\), all original denominators are nonzero, and \\(ab+1>0\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{a+b^{-1}}{a^{-1}+b}\n=\\frac{(ab+1)/b}{(ab+1)/a}\n=\\frac ab.\n\\]\nThe given equation is consequently equivalent to \\(a=13b\\). The bound on the sum becomes\n\\[a+b=14b\\le100.\\]\nBecause \\(14\\cdot7=98\\) and \\(14\\cdot8=112\\), the possible positive integers are exactly \\(b=1,2,\\ldots,7\\).\n\nEach such \\(b\\) gives one distinct pair \\((13b,b)\\), and every valid pair must have this form. Hence there are \\(\\boxed{7}\\) pairs, choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
